From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: the signal context is FreeBSD's, and it can be read

⚠ THIS REVERSES patch 0001, WHICH WAS RIGHT WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN AND IS NOT ANY MORE. 0001 turned
context_from_segfault/context_to_segfault into no-ops and deleted the fastmem rewrite from
core/linux/common.cpp, on the grounds that "the offset of rip in what actually arrives has been
dumped on hardware without being identified". Two things have happened since.

⚠ FIRST, SA_SIGINFO NOW WORKS, AND IT NEVER WAS A KERNEL LIMITATION. The port's conclusion, from a
Play! crash, was "this kernel does not deliver SA_SIGINFO": a SIGSEGV arrived with rdi = 11,
rsi = 2 and a stack address in rdx - FreeBSD's original (int sig, int code, struct sigcontext *scp)
rather than SA_SIGINFO's three. The cause was the flag VALUE. The SDK's bits/signal.h says
SA_SIGINFO is 4; this kernel is FreeBSD's and reads 4 as SA_RESETHAND, so every handler in this
port was installed one-shot and without siginfo. orbis-compat/include/signal.h now defines the
FreeBSD numbers - SA_SIGINFO 0x0040, SA_ONSTACK 0x0001 - and cores built by this harness get that
header first on the include path. The proof is on hardware, 2026-08-29:

    fatal: signal 11 - SIGSEGV (bad address), si_code 2, fault address 0x803f61000

- a real si_code and a real si_addr, from a handler that had printed neither in the whole life of
this project. So `act.sa_flags = SA_SIGINFO` in os_InstallFaultHandler now asks for what it says,
and fault_handler's `si->si_addr` is an address rather than a small integer read as a pointer.

⚠ SECOND, THE mcontext LAYOUT WAS MEASURED. orbis-compat/src/orbis_boot.cpp needed the same thing
for its crash reporter and found it, on hardware, 2026-08-30: the words behind the third argument
are FreeBSD's amd64 mcontext, starting 64 bytes in rather than the 16 a bare FreeBSD ucontext_t
would account for. mc_trapno, mc_addr, mc_rip, mc_rflags and mc_rsp were all read at FreeBSD's
offsets from that base and all five agreed with the fault they described.

⚠ AND THE OFFSET IS STILL NOT COMPILED IN, FOR THE REASON 0001 GAVE. A number measured once is not
a contract. So this uses orbis_boot.cpp's own answer: a user thread on amd64 has fixed segment
selectors - mc_cs 0x43, mc_ss 0x3b, three words apart in FreeBSD's mcontext - and finding that pair
anchors every other field, on whatever prefix this kernel happens to put in front of it.

WHY IT MATTERS, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT: nvmem is not optional-fastmem plus a fallback. The x64
recompiler emits `call MemHandlers[Fast]` for EVERY dynamic memory access - which is most of them,
since @Rn, @(R0,Rn) and @-Rn are how SH4 code touches memory - and the Fast handler dereferences
ram_base+addr with no check at all. Accesses that land on a hardware register instead of RAM are
SUPPOSED to fault, once per call site, so that rewriteMemAccess can patch that site to the Slow
handler. Without the rewrite the first such access is fatal. A memory backend without this patch
would not be slow, it would be a core that dies on the first MMIO write through a pointer.

⚠ THE GUARDS ARE NOT DECORATION. rewriteMemAccess reads the return address off context.rsp and
ASSEMBLES A CALL over it. A context that is merely plausible would write x86 into whatever that
pointed at, which is worse than having no fastmem. So common.cpp asks twice - the selector anchor
must have been found (pc != 0) AND mc_addr must equal si_addr - and does nothing at all otherwise.

Left deliberately unproven, because nothing on this console has tested it: whether this kernel
honours a MODIFIED context on return from a handler. If it does not, the same rip and the same
si_addr arrive a second time, and common.cpp says so once before the death that follows.

diff --git a/core/linux/context.cpp b/core/linux/context.cpp
--- a/core/linux/context.cpp
+++ b/core/linux/context.cpp
@@ -36,6 +36,85 @@
 		ctx = (Tctx)seg;
 }
 
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+// ⚠ THE LAYOUT IS FOUND AT RUN TIME, NOT LOOKED UP, AND THE REASON IS THAT BOTH HEADERS LIE.
+// This SDK ships musl's <bits/signal.h> - an mcontext_t of gregs[23] whose REG_RIP is behind
+// #ifdef _GNU_SOURCE - while the kernel on the other side of the syscall is FreeBSD's and writes
+// FreeBSD's amd64 mcontext. The __unix__ arm below therefore fails to COMPILE (`use of undeclared
+// identifier 'REG_RIP'`) and the __FreeBSD__ arm above would not compile either, because the
+// declared struct has no mc_rip to name. Naming a field here is not on offer at all.
+//
+// ⚠ AND THE OFFSET IS NOT A CONSTANT TO PASTE IN EITHER. What arrives is not a bare FreeBSD
+// ucontext_t: the mcontext sits 64 bytes in rather than the 16 that FreeBSD's own uc_sigmask
+// would account for, so a compiled-in offset would be one measurement standing in for a contract
+// nobody has. orbis-compat/src/orbis_boot.cpp solved this for the crash reporter and this is the
+// same solution: a user thread on amd64 has FIXED SEGMENT SELECTORS - mc_cs is 0x43 and mc_ss is
+// 0x3b - and in FreeBSD's mcontext they sit exactly three words apart. Finding that pair anchors
+// every other field. Measured on hardware 2026-08-30: mc_ss landed at ctx[32], which puts the
+// mcontext at ctx[8] and mc_rip at ctx[28].
+//
+// ⚠ AND THE FLAG THAT MAKES ANY OF THIS REACHABLE IS A DIFFERENT BUG, ALREADY FIXED ELSEWHERE.
+// The SDK's SA_SIGINFO is Linux's 4, which this kernel reads as SA_RESETHAND - so os_
+// InstallFaultHandler used to install a ONE-SHOT handler WITHOUT siginfo, and the handler was
+// called with FreeBSD's original (int sig, int code, struct sigcontext *scp). That is where
+// "this kernel does not deliver SA_SIGINFO" came from, and it was wrong: orbis-compat/include/
+// signal.h now defines SA_SIGINFO as FreeBSD's 0x0040 and the crash reporter has since printed a
+// real si_code and si_addr on hardware. Cores built by this port get that header first on the
+// include path, so `act.sa_flags = SA_SIGINFO` below in common.cpp asks for what it says.
+//
+// ⚠ mc_r9 AND mc_rdi WERE NOT SEPARATELY MEASURED, AND THAT IS FINE FOR A REASON WORTH STATING.
+// They are not independent guesses: the anchor fixes the base of ONE struct, and mc_trapno,
+// mc_addr, mc_rip, mc_rflags and mc_rsp were then all read at FreeBSD's offsets from that base
+// and all five agreed with what the fault actually was. A layout that matches at five separated
+// points and is wrong at two interior ones is not a thing structs do. The caller checks a sixth
+// anchor anyway before acting - see orbis_segfault_context_agrees below.
+namespace {
+
+// Field order of FreeBSD's amd64 mcontext_t, in 8-byte words from the start of the struct.
+enum : int {
+	MC_RDI  = 1,
+	MC_R9   = 6,
+	MC_ADDR = 17,
+	MC_RIP  = 20,
+	MC_CS   = 21,
+	MC_RSP  = 23,
+	MC_SS   = 24
+};
+
+// Word index at which the mcontext starts inside the pointer the kernel handed the handler, or
+// -1 if the selector pair is not there - which means this is not FreeBSD's amd64 mcontext and
+// nothing below may be read under a field name.
+int orbisMcontextBase(const void *segfault_ctx)
+{
+	if (segfault_ctx == nullptr)
+		return -1;
+	const u64 *w = (const u64 *)segfault_ctx;
+	// Far past mc_ss for any plausible prefix. Reading this much of the pointer is what
+	// orbis_boot.cpp's dump already does on hardware without faulting.
+	const int words = 96;
+	for (int i = MC_CS; i + (MC_SS - MC_CS) < words; i++)
+		if (w[i] == 0x43u && w[i + (MC_SS - MC_CS)] == 0x3bu)
+			return i - MC_CS;
+	return -1;
+}
+
+}
+
+// ⚠ THE SECOND, INDEPENDENT ANCHOR, AND THE CALLER MUST ASK BEFORE IT TRUSTS A REWRITE.
+// siginfo already carries the faulting address, so mc_addr has to equal si_addr. A context that
+// passes the selector test and fails this one is a coincidence in someone else's stack frame, and
+// acting on it would mean assembling x86 over whatever `rsp` happened to point at. Same
+// discipline as orbis_boot.cpp: two anchors, or the honest answer that we do not know.
+extern "C" bool orbis_segfault_context_agrees(void *segfault_ctx, void *fault_addr)
+{
+	const int mc = orbisMcontextBase(segfault_ctx);
+	if (mc < 0)
+		return false;
+	const u64 *w = (const u64 *)segfault_ctx;
+	return w[mc + MC_ADDR] == (u64)(uintptr_t)fault_addr;
+}
+#endif // __ORBIS__
+
 template<bool ToSegfault>
 static void context_segfault(host_context_t* hostctx, void* segfault_ctx)
 {
@@ -98,27 +177,35 @@
 	#endif
 #elif HOST_CPU == CPU_X64
 	#if defined(__ORBIS__)
-		// ⚠ THIS CONSOLE'S SIGNAL CONTEXT LAYOUT IS NOT KNOWN, SO NO REGISTER IS READ HERE.
-		// The SDK ships musl's <bits/signal.h>: a mcontext_t of gregs[23] with REG_RIP and
-		// friends behind #ifdef _GNU_SOURCE, which is not defined on this build - hence the
-		// `use of undeclared identifier 'REG_RIP'` that the __unix__ arm below fails with. The
-		// FreeBSD arm above cannot run either, and not only because the SDK's libc++ __config
-		// does `#undef __FreeBSD__` before this file gets here: that same mcontext_t has no
-		// mc_rip to name.
-		//
-		// So the declared layout is Linux's and the kernel is FreeBSD's, and the port has
-		// already measured that the two do not agree - orbis-compat's crash handler dumps
-		// thirty-two words of this very pointer and reports that NONE of them was a text
-		// address, which is why it falls back to backtrace() for the faulting instruction.
-		// Until that offset is measured and written down, any field named here would be a
-		// guess dressed up as a register.
-		//
-		// The caller in core/linux/common.cpp is patched to match: the fastmem rewrite is the
-		// only thing that reads this context, and it does not run on this platform. A zeroed
-		// host_context_t handed to sh4Dynarec->rewrite() would be worse than no fastmem - it
-		// would patch code at pc 0.
-		(void)hostctx;
-		(void)segfault_ctx;
+		// The words are FreeBSD's amd64 mcontext, anchored at run time - see orbisMcontextBase
+		// above for why the offset can come neither from a header nor from a constant.
+		{
+			const int mc = orbisMcontextBase(segfault_ctx);
+			if (mc < 0)
+			{
+				// ⚠ pc = 0 IS THE SIGNAL TO THE CALLER, AND IT HAS TO BE ONE IT CANNOT MISS.
+				// core/linux/common.cpp refuses to rewrite on a zero pc. A host_context_t left
+				// uninitialised, or zeroed only in part, would be handed to sh4Dynarec->rewrite()
+				// and would patch code at whatever `rsp` happened to hold - which is worse than
+				// having no fastmem at all, and is the failure this arm used to avoid by reading
+				// nothing whatsoever.
+				if (!ToSegfault)
+				{
+					hostctx->pc  = 0;
+					hostctx->rsp = 0;
+					hostctx->r9  = 0;
+					hostctx->rdi = 0;
+				}
+			}
+			else
+			{
+				u64 *w = (u64 *)segfault_ctx;
+				bicopy<ToSegfault>(hostctx->pc,  w[mc + MC_RIP]);
+				bicopy<ToSegfault>(hostctx->rsp, w[mc + MC_RSP]);
+				bicopy<ToSegfault>(hostctx->r9,  w[mc + MC_R9]);
+				bicopy<ToSegfault>(hostctx->rdi, w[mc + MC_RDI]);
+			}
+		}
 	#elif defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__DragonFly__)
 		bicopy<ToSegfault>(hostctx->pc, MCTX(.mc_rip));
 		bicopy<ToSegfault>(hostctx->rsp, MCTX(.mc_rsp));
diff --git a/core/linux/common.cpp b/core/linux/common.cpp
--- a/core/linux/common.cpp
+++ b/core/linux/common.cpp
@@ -33,6 +33,12 @@
 void context_from_segfault(host_context_t* hctx, void* segfault_ctx);
 void context_to_segfault(host_context_t* hctx, void* segfault_ctx);
 
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+// Defined in core/linux/context.cpp. True only when the words behind segfault_ctx really are
+// FreeBSD's amd64 mcontext AND its mc_addr agrees with the address siginfo reported.
+extern "C" bool orbis_segfault_context_agrees(void *segfault_ctx, void *fault_addr);
+#endif
+
 #ifndef __SWITCH__
 static struct sigaction next_segv_handler;
 #endif
@@ -56,26 +62,65 @@
 	if (addrspace::bm_lockedWrite((u8*)si->si_addr))
 		return;
 
-#ifndef __ORBIS__
 	// fast mem access rewriting
 	host_context_t ctx;
 	context_from_segfault(&ctx, segfault_ctx);
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	// ⚠ TWO GUARDS BEFORE A REWRITE, AND NEITHER IS OPTIONAL ON THIS CONSOLE.
+	//
+	// context.cpp's __ORBIS__ arm finds FreeBSD's mcontext inside the kernel's context by hunting
+	// for the fixed selector pair (mc_cs 0x43, mc_ss 0x3b) rather than by naming a field, because
+	// this SDK declares musl's mcontext and the kernel writes FreeBSD's. A pc of 0 is that search
+	// having failed. The second guard is the independent one: mc_addr must equal the address
+	// siginfo reported, or the anchor landed on a coincidence somewhere else in the stack.
+	//
+	// Getting this wrong is not "no fastmem". rewriteMemAccess reads the return address off
+	// context.rsp and ASSEMBLES A CALL over it, so a context we merely believe would write x86
+	// into whatever that pointed at.
+	//
+	// ⚠ AND THE THIRD FAILURE IS THE ONE WITH NO ERROR CODE: whether this kernel honours a
+	// MODIFIED context on return. FreeBSD's trampoline sigreturn()s the same ucontext the handler
+	// was given, which is how every other platform here resumes - but nothing in this port has
+	// observed it working on Orbis yet. If it silently does not, the faulting instruction
+	// re-executes, the call site is already patched to Slow, rewriteMemAccess finds no Fast
+	// handler to match and dies inside `die("Failed to match the code")` - a message that names
+	// the wrong thing. So the pair is remembered and a second identical arrival says what
+	// actually happened, once, before the same death.
+	static uintptr_t last_pc;
+	static void     *last_addr;
+	const bool trustworthy = ctx.pc != 0
+	                      && orbis_segfault_context_agrees(segfault_ctx, si->si_addr);
+	if (trustworthy && ctx.pc == last_pc && si->si_addr == last_addr)
+	{
+		ERROR_LOG(COMMON, "SIGSEGV at rip %p on %p for the SECOND time. The rewrite was applied "
+				"and the resumed context did not take it, which means this kernel does not honour "
+				"a modified ucontext on return from a signal handler. nvmem cannot work here; the "
+				"next line is the consequence, not the cause.",
+				(void *)ctx.pc, si->si_addr);
+	}
+	else if (trustworthy)
+	{
+		last_pc   = ctx.pc;
+		last_addr = si->si_addr;
+		if (sh4Dynarec->rewrite(ctx, si->si_addr))
+		{
+			context_to_segfault(&ctx, segfault_ctx);
+			return;
+		}
+	}
+	else
+		ERROR_LOG(COMMON, "SIGSEGV on %p and the signal context could not be anchored "
+				"(mc_cs/mc_ss pair %s) - not rewriting anything from it",
+				si->si_addr, ctx.pc != 0 ? "found, but mc_addr disagrees with si_addr" : "NOT found");
+#else
 	if (sh4Dynarec->rewrite(ctx, si->si_addr))
 	{
 		context_to_segfault(&ctx, segfault_ctx);
 		return;
 	}
+#endif
 
 	ERROR_LOG(COMMON, "SIGSEGV @ %p invalid access to %p", (void *)ctx.pc, si->si_addr);
-#else
-	// ⚠ NO REWRITE HERE, BECAUSE THERE IS NO CONTEXT TO REWRITE FROM. core/linux/context.cpp's
-	// __ORBIS__ arm says it in full: this SDK declares musl's mcontext_t while the kernel
-	// passes FreeBSD's, and the offset of rip in what actually arrives has been dumped on
-	// hardware without being identified. Everything above this point still works - the
-	// rollback watcher, the RAM and VRAM write protection and the FPCB lock all key on
-	// si->si_addr alone - so what is lost is the fastmem fixup, not fault handling.
-	ERROR_LOG(COMMON, "SIGSEGV invalid access to %p", si->si_addr);
-#endif
 #endif
 
 #ifdef __SWITCH__
